WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.930
LINDSAY: Good morning, everyone.

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:03.750
Welcome to the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:09.120
I am Jim Lindsay, senior vice president and
director of studies here at the Council.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:16.430
It is my great pleasure to open today's symposium
on The New Geopolitics of China, India, and

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.780
Pakistan.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.160
It's a crucial issue.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.630
And I want to thank all of you for taking
the time to be here today.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:30.590
I want to give an extra-special thanks to
everyone who had to take more than a car ride

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:37.679
or a Metro ride or a cab ride to get here,
particularly to our speakers, five of whom

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:41.950
traveled all the way from Asia to join in
the conversation.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:47.499
Having just recently come back from Singapore,
I know it's quite a hike to make that trip.

00:00:47.499 --> 00:00:51.899
So I hope the jet lag isn't kicking in at
this moment.

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:56.429
I'd like to remind everybody that today's
event is on the record.

00:00:56.429 --> 00:01:01.569
As Stacy (sp) mentioned, we will be streaming
it online to all those people who are watching

00:01:01.569 --> 00:01:03.600
us from their computers and laptops.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.600
Hello.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:05.600
Welcome.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.680
After we conclude today, the video will be
available on CFR's YouTube page.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:17.979
And you are welcome-actually, you're encouraged
to live-tweet today's event using the hashtag

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:20.689
#NewGeopolitics.

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:27.950
Now, today's symposium comes at a time of
increasingly complex and fraught relations

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:31.850
among China, India, and Pakistan.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:37.640
Tensions have been rising throughout Southern
Asia, raising concerns about the eruption

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.140
of conflict.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:44.729
So today's panels will examine current regional
flashpoints with an eye toward steps each

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:50.920
of the countries, as well as the United States,
could take to dampen tensions and advance

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.909
regional stability.

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:57.689
But Southern Asia isn't just a place about
potential conflict.

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:02.460
It is also a place where the benefits of cooperation
are potentially immense.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:08.709
So today's panels will also be assessing opportunities
to improve regional economic integration and

00:02:08.709 --> 00:02:10.039
exchange.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:17.730
Now, today's symposium caps a two-year collaborative
project examining the evolving relationships

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.380
among China, India, and Pakistan.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:26.180
Today's event, as well as the larger project,
has been made possible through the generous

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:29.410
support of the MacArthur Foundation.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:33.390
On behalf of the Council, I'd like to thank
the MacArthur Foundation for its continued

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:38.250
support of our work and for making today's
symposium possible.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:43.989
I also want to recognize and thank three of
my colleagues in CFR's Asia Studies program

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:47.650
for running the project and designing today's
panels.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:53.459
They are Alyssa Ayres, senior fellow for India,
Pakistan, and South Asia-she directed the

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:55.870
project; Elizabeth Economy, who is the C.V.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:02.060
Starr senior fellow and director of our Asia
Studies program; and Dan Markey, our adjunct

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.310
senior fellow for Pakistan and South Asia.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:12.000
They have done a terrific job shepherding
the project overall and pulling together today's

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.000
event.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.599
So I want to say thanks to them.

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:23.659
Thanks are also due to CFR's meeting team,
especially to Dexter Ndengabaganizi, as well

00:03:23.659 --> 00:03:28.700
as to Ashlyn Anderson in the studies program,
for all their hard work in planning today's

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:29.700
symposium.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:37.400
I also want to thank CFR's events team, which
is led by Rachel Lumpkin, for making everything

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.700
run so smoothly.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.090
And I think you're in for a very good event
today.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:47.930
We are lucky to begin with a keynote panel
of former ambassadors who can tell us in very

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:53.049
practical terms what the opportunities and
challenges look like on the ground.

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:57.730
And again, given the incredible speakers we
have here today, I have no doubt that this

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.690
will be a productive and informative series
of discussions.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:06.019
And so, with that, I will turn it over to
Ambassador Rick Inderfurth, former assistant

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:11.709
secretary of state for South Asia, who will
moderate our first session, which is Major

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:14.129
Flashpoints in Southern Asia.

00:04:14.129 --> 00:04:15.569
Rick, over to you.

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:19.609
I invite everybody to come up to the stage.

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:33.100
INDERFURTH: Good to see you, Jim.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:44.340
LINDSAY: Good to see you.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:55.580
INDERFURTH: Well, good morning, everyone.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:05:02.550
Thanks to Jim for his introductory remarks
about the symposium and our panel this morning.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.090
As he said, I am Rick Inderfurth.

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:05.810
I'm also formerly known as Karl.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:07.639
I apologize for the confusion.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:10.150
I actually answer to both.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:12.930
So that's the case.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:18.639
I am very pleased to be a part of this great
panel that we have this morning.

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:22.250
We have certain time limits, so we're going
to move rather rapidly through this.

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.930
I think we will be ending at 9:45.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:32.400
So we're going to try to march through this
and get as much of an exchange as we can.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.759
Our topic is Major Flashpoints in Southern
Asia.

00:05:35.759 --> 00:05:43.580
And our focus will be on current transformations
of Asia's strategic landscape and how the

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:49.889
return of geopolitics affects regional and
peace-regional peace and security issues.

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:54.889
Now, that's a lot of terrain to cover in a
short period of time, but I think that fortunately

00:05:54.889 --> 00:05:59.410
we have four panelists, excellent panelists,
who will be able to make the most of the time

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:00.530
we have together.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:05.009
I'd like to give you a brief introduction
of our four panelists.

00:06:05.009 --> 00:06:11.620
Samina Ahmed is project director for South
Asia for the International Crisis Group.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:19.370
Her focus is on the risks of extremism, terrorism,
internal conflict, and war.

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:25.150
To paraphrase Samantha Powers' book, these
truly are the problems from hell.

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:27.270
So I sympathize.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:34.550
Daniel Markey is a senior adjunct fellow here
at the Council for India, Pakistan, and South

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:40.949
Asia and a research professor at Johns Hopkins
School of Advanced International Studies.

00:06:40.949 --> 00:06:44.660
Previously he served at the State Department
on the policy planning staff.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.720
And I think, Dan, that's where you and I first
crossed paths.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:51.190
So good to see you again.

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:56.090
Raja Mohan is our next panelist.

00:06:56.090 --> 00:07:03.130
He is the director of Carnegie India and is
one of India's leading strategic thinkers.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:08.699
Fittingly, when he was here in Washington
at the Library of Congress, he held the Henry

00:07:08.699 --> 00:07:15.060
A. Kissinger chair in foreign policy, who
also, as we know, is another great strategic

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.060
thinker.

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:19.509
You're in good company, Raja.

00:07:19.509 --> 00:07:24.919
Raja is also a member of India's prestigious
National Security Advisory Board.

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:32.840
And Wang Xu is executive-our final speaker-executive
deputy director of the Center for South Asian

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:35.660
Studies at Peking University.

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:42.849
Dr. Wang has spent a lot of his time living
and working in Pakistan and has devoted considerable

00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:45.849
attention to the Chinese One Belt, One Road
initiative.

00:07:45.849 --> 00:07:51.100
I've often wondered if perhaps one day the
One Belt, One Road initiative might intersect

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:58.310
with the U.S. Silk Road initiative, but I
guess we'll have to see if that takes place.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:07:59.699
So those are our panelists.

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:04.520
You have their fuller bios in the material
in your packets.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:11.830
So I'm going to start off with a conversation
for approximately the next 20, 30 minutes

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:19.360
with our panelists, posing three various questions
to them.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:26.919
And then we will begin the open conversation
with the members here until 9:45.

00:08:26.919 --> 00:08:33.270
So to start, to start-a little bit early in
the morning for a broad geopolitical discussion,

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:37.800
but I think we will-hopefully you've got enough
coffee here to get you awake and moving.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:44.050
I'd like to start with a, as I said, a broad
question, the 20,000-foot question to begin.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:48.980
And I'd like to direct this to our panelists
from India and China, Dr. Mohan and Dr. Wang.

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:55.331
I want to take you back to a report by the
National Intelligence Council, the U.S. National

00:08:55.331 --> 00:09:03.890
Intelligence Council, in 2004, entitled "Mapping
the Global Future."

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:09.221
In that report, the NIC, as it's called, made
this prediction, and I will quote it.

00:09:09.221 --> 00:09:17.790
It said, "The likely emergence of China and
India as new major global players"-and now

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:24.070
get this point-"similar to the advent of a
united Germany in the 19th century and a powerful

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:31.420
United States in the 20th century, will transform
the geopolitical landscape with impacts potentially

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:36.530
as dramatic as those in the previous two centuries."

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:39.650
That's quite a statement.

00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:43.860
It's now been 12 years since the NIC made
that prediction.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:48.940
And so that leads me to my first question
for Dr. Mohan and Dr. Wang.

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:56.450
The question is, is this transformation of
the geopolitical landscape in Asia, as predicted

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:59.230
by the National Intelligence Council, taking
place?

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:02.910
And, if so, in what ways?

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:06.940
And what impact is it having on regional peace
and security issues?

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.510
Raja, why don't we start with you?

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:10.510
Good.

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:15.450
MOHAN: I think what was a prediction then
is a reality now, certainly with respect to

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:21.750
China, which has, over the last decade, has
emerged as the second-largest economy.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:25.360
And consequentially its weight in the region
has dramatically increased.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:30.660
It's nearly now the largest trading partner
for all of South Asia, if not for much of

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:32.270
the world.

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:35.540
It's also begun to replace U.S.-led institutions.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:42.830
They've certainly created new ones; for example,
like the Asian Infrastructure Investment bank.

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:47.490
Along with India, they have the new development
bank under the BRICS formula.

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:53.780
So on the economic side, today China's weight
in the region is quite significant compared

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:59.110
to-for example, you mentioned the intersection
between Clinton's proposal of a new Silk Road

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:00.670
and the China's OBOR.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:01.670
There's no intersection.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:05.960
I mean, China has just overwhelmed the American
proposal, because the U.S. has a proposal

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:11.220
but there's no money to back it, while the
Chinese today are bringing in money and can

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.810
deliver projects in two years, three years,
short order, depending on how cooperative

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:19.760
you might be with the-with the Chinese companies
and institutions.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:24.160
So I think it's really transformative in terms
of what they're doing, in terms of the connectivity

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:25.330
within the region.

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:29.490
There's also backlash to it in some places,
like Sri Lanka, where some of the projects

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:32.480
have been challenged for their financial conditions,
et cetera.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:37.030
So, but, for good or bad, China's economic
impact is quite dramatic.

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:41.260
Second thing I would say, the fact that the
U.S. talks about a pivot to East Asia but

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:48.790
is eager to work with the Americans in Afghanistan
tells you the relative weight of the Chinese

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:49.810
influence in the region.

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:53.490
It tells you that even as you compete with
them elsewhere, you've got to work with them

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:55.080
within the region.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:59.670
So both on the political and economic side,
there is-the Chinese weight today is quite

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:00.670
significant.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.740
India's rise has been less dramatic, less
consequential than the Chinese.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:10.570
But there is a growing agency for India today,
that India can actually shape quite a bit

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:12.800
of the outcomes within the region.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:17.500
I'll conclude by saying that 25 years ago,
at the end of the Cold War, it looked like

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:24.790
U.S. has the authority, power, and the resources
to define the environment in the region.

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:31.640
Today, that capacity is being questioned,
both because of the will in Washington as

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:36.130
well as the capacity to do things there, and
that China and India today can do a lot more

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:40.900
to shape the regional environment from a time
when we were merely passive players to one

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:47.270
today, what we do will make a difference to
what the U.S. does.

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:53.130
WANG: Both China and India has two promising
new economy.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:58.050
Definitely there will be huge expectations
on China and India when they are transforming

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:01.120
about geopolitics in the region.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:06.640
But I'm afraid that, according to my humble
understanding, both China and India, as my

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:12.250
friend Raja Mohan just mentioned, I mean,
a little bit overestimated about our influence.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:19.120
I think, at current state, both China and
India could be more influential on the regional

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:20.120
issues.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:24.440
Second point I want to mention is that I think
both China and India, the engagement-more

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:30.760
engagement or more close agreement between
India and China could help the regional stability

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:31.760
a lot.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:37.810
For example, we have-both our new governments
have this strong political willingness for

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:39.870
betterizing our relations.

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:47.920
But given some historical issues, it still
needs a long way to have a more better relation.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:52.200
But still, we are focusing, as you just mentioned,
about the Belt and Road initiative.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:55.010
I think it's not like China buying something.

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:58.970
But, for example, everybody knows that in
now, China, we have a slowed-down Chinese

00:13:58.970 --> 00:13:59.970
economy.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:01.190
We have the new norms.

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:06.240
But, at the same time, India has a promising
potential rapid economic growth.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:11.940
So why not, let's say, converge the Chinese
industrial cooperation with India and make

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:20.510
these two huge both manufacturers and also
two big consuming markets to be a better future,

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:22.100
regional economic integration?

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:23.130
That will help a lot.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:28.410
The third point I want to mention, that more
engagement between China and India might be

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.810
a (rising-sum ?) thinking or concerns.

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:37.100
But given the lessons learned from the last,
we have several rounds-more than seven rounds

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:40.540
of the counterterrorism dialogues between
India and China.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:44.960
And also we have several rounds of the border
discussions.

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:50.980
So I think that it could help at least-even
if we can't solve the problems in short time,

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:56.810
but we can have a better crisis management,
so that we can help the regional stability

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:57.810
a lot.

00:14:57.810 --> 00:14:58.810
INDERFURTH: Good.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:05.820
Let me just pursue a couple of things, because
this focus on this panel is on the major flashpoints

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:12.590
in Southern Asia and the question of the transformation
of the geopolitical landscape of rising China

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:16.550
and India on the issues relating to peace
and security.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:20.770
I'd like you both to comment a little bit
more about that from the standpoint of the

00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:22.270
two.

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:31.240
We all know that in 1962 there was a defining
moment of the Sino-Indian border war, which

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:36.780
is still a lingering legacy today in the relationship.

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:43.650
And more recently we have-and I'm not Raja's
book agent, but we have this wonderful account

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:53.060
that Raja has written on Samudra Manthan,
Sino rivalry-Sino-Indian rivalry in the Indo-Pacific.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:58.960
Both countries are moving forward with naval
modernization, a blue-water navy, the whole

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:03.780
issue of not only the Indian Ocean but the
South China Sea.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:05.410
You knew this was coming, I'm sure.

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:11.450
There will be many questions during the audience
participation on that, I would expect.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:17.160
But where is the-in terms of transforming
the geopolitical landscape in the security

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:20.860
realm, where is that heading right now?

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:24.220
Raja, do you want to start as-
MOHAN: Yeah.

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:27.490
INDERFURTH: And do you want to explain the
title of your book?

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:31.500
I've often said this is one of the best titles
that I've seen.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:34.760
Using a Hindu fable as a title of a book is
pretty good.

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:37.930
MOHAN: My wife always scolded me, saying that
nobody's going to understand that.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:38.930
(Laughter.)

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:39.930
IT's not really said, so.

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:43.330
But nevertheless, I think it talks about the
churning of the oceans.

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:50.340
So that is the fable where actually the angels
and the demons tried to fight for the kind

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:52.890
of nectar or whatever it is.

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:56.730
And in that fight, actually a third party,
the lord himself, intervenes and it tilts

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:58.160
to one side.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:02.710
Some people have accused me of comparing the
god to the United States, but a lot's happened

00:17:02.710 --> 00:17:03.720
since then.

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:04.720
(Laughter.)

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:05.950
But the fact is this.

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:11.521
I mean, the-I think the key point there is,
as a rising economic power-powers, if you

00:17:11.521 --> 00:17:15.030
will-both India and China are going to build
large navies.

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:19.780
I think, whether we like it or not-I mean,
it's not a-it's like all the great trading

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:24.350
powers of the past, that China and India today
are going to build navies.

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:29.450
Much like Portugal, Netherlands, France, Britain,
and the United States, who came to the Indian

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:33.590
Ocean, the Chinese are going to come to the
Indian Ocean, whether we like it or not.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:35.240
So it's inevitable.

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:37.070
The only thing is they're coming from the
other direction.

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:39.590
Instead of coming from the west, they come
from the east.

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:44.860
And India is going to build its naval capabilities,
seek to consolidate its primacy in the Indian

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:50.190
Ocean, and we'll also try and go in partnership
with the Americans and the Japanese to the

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:51.730
Pacific Ocean.

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:54.450
And China, meanwhile, is creating partnerships.

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:58.310
They've just announced the construction of
some new bases in the Indian Ocean.

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:02.120
There was a time when we used to say, look,
bases-remember Diego Garcia and all that stuff?

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:06.540
But we love to do it ourselves these days,
because I think it's in the scheme of things.

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:10.190
If you want a big navy that must operate beyond
your shores, you need facilities.

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:11.660
You need special relationships.

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:12.770
You need access.

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:16.430
I think both of us are doing that, and I think
that's going to bring some friction.

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:22.450
I mean, the Chinese submarines show up in
Sri Lanka, so there is delegates upset.

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:26.360
Indians are training the Vietnamese submarine
crews, so that upsets the Beijing.

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:30.080
But this is going to be just the beginning
of a-of a friction that this book talks about.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:32.820
But the question is, we need to live with
that.

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:34.400
We need to learn to manage that.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:38.470
And the question is, can we work together
to limit some of the damage?

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:39.470
That is a big question.

00:18:39.470 --> 00:18:46.420
But in the interim, the Chinese partnership
with Pakistan on the naval side is of great

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:47.460
concern to India.

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:52.100
You know, India is concerned about Chinese
navy tying up with some of her neighbors.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:53.450
So those issues are going to come to the fore.

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:54.590
We're just not there yet.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:57.230
But I think it's a question of time.

00:18:57.230 --> 00:18:58.930
So the book talks about a security dilemma.

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:02.550
The question is, then, do we find ways to
manage it?

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:07.560
But I think the maritime dimension remains
to be explored, because we've been so obsessed

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:12.930
with the Himalayas, both in India, Pakistan,
China, and the United States, that I think

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:17.180
it's time to focus on what's happening in
the seas and how that has a bigger potential

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:20.160
to shape the regional environment in Southern
Asia.

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.260
INDERFURTH: Dr. Wang.

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:30.761
WANG: I think this question is quite interesting,
because as we just mentioned, that back in

00:19:30.761 --> 00:19:34.720
China-I'm not speaking for the government,
but definitely I will say something about

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:36.140
this.

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:42.940
For example, we have-we have another thinking
of the-not zero-sum games.

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:50.790
We believe that the writing of the Chinese-even
the military power is not as threatening to

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:54.600
the peace of the world, but to keep the world
at more peace.

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:58.480
Secondly-(chuckles)-secondly, I think you
mentioned about very complicated issues between

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:00.700
China, Pakistan, and India.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:08.350
I think what our new leadership has described
as-our President Xi made a speech in-(inaudible)-during

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:11.610
his visit in 2014.

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:19.040
He (restated ?) the strategic importance of
India to our-Chinese policy towards South

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:20.040
Asia.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:25.560
At the same time, we still want our old friend
Pakistan to be satisfied with our progress

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:31.620
with India, more engagement with India.

00:20:31.620 --> 00:20:35.300
It is not, let's say, China-India better,
China-Pakistan worse.

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:42.060
No, it's a-(inaudible)-better harmony, we
call this, or we call this a community of

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:43.140
common destiny.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:48.860
The second point I want to mention is that
we have a large oversea(s) investment nowadays,

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:51.350
and we have a large population of the tourists.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:57.370
So definitely, back in Beijing or back in
China, it is very natural or very rational,

00:20:57.370 --> 00:21:02.150
logical concern about how the Chinese government
to protect at the same time when we have a

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:04.510
big economic power.

00:21:04.510 --> 00:21:08.650
So the government has to think more about
the responsibility of protecting overseas

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:11.470
investment, also the personnels.

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:17.260
So definitely the existence or the appearance
of the Chinese navy in Indian Ocean should

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:22.210
not be regarded as a threat to, let's say,
the Indian people or something.

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:30.590
(Inaudible)-I think we are-as I just mentioned,
that we are more focusing on geoeconomic purpose.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:37.620
But in South Asia, we are facing quite security
threats; I mean, the nontraditional security

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:41.770
threats, either the piracies or the terrorist
attacks.

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:48.400
So on the-maritime cooperation between China
and India could help this region, even the

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:50.750
Indian Ocean region, to be a more stable one.

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:51.750
INDERFURTH: Excellent.

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:52.750
Dan?

00:21:52.750 --> 00:21:56.030
MARKEY: Yeah, if I could, just on this point,
because it relates very directly to one of

00:21:56.030 --> 00:22:02.600
the projects or one of the sub-projects that's
a part of this MacArthur project as a whole.

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:10.550
I worked on a research agenda which was really
focused on the question of could we see a

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:17.250
China-India flashpoint in the relative near
term, say, the next 12 to 18 months?

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:22.930
And there generally the take-home was this
is a relationship that, in that timeframe,

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:25.400
is actually remarkably stable.

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:32.870
I think a lot of the kind of cooperative dynamics
that Dr. Wang has pointed out dominate certainly

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:34.710
in this timeframe.

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:40.980
But as Raja has pointed out, if you look out
further, a lot of the competitive instincts

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:43.960
on both sides may come to the fore.

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:49.140
So the conclusion of this piece was there
were basically four potential flashpoints

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:51.590
that could come up in the near term.

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:57.590
Not one of them on its own was likely to bring
India and China to blows.

00:22:57.590 --> 00:23:01.790
But if you had to put your finger on one,
it would be the land border.

00:23:01.790 --> 00:23:07.480
But if you had a compounded crisis-that is,
more than one crisis looming at roughly the

00:23:07.480 --> 00:23:11.680
same time-then things might become less manageable.

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:18.590
And so the potential candidates there were
upheaval related to Tibet, a maritime dispute

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:25.170
related to Raja's point, and a potential India-Pakistan
crisis that took place at roughly the same

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:26.860
time as a China-India crisis.

00:23:26.860 --> 00:23:32.180
If you saw this merger of crises, then you
could begin to get into some very dangerous

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:35.190
territory even within the next 12 to 18 months.

00:23:35.190 --> 00:23:40.540
But still, at the end, we had to put this
in the category of plausible but quite unlikely.

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:42.380
INDERFURTH: Great.

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:48.020
Wonderful segue to the second question I'd
like to pose to all of our panelists regarding

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:54.320
a major flashpoint in Southern Asia today,
and that is Afghanistan.

00:23:54.320 --> 00:24:00.850
As we know, Pakistan, India, and China all
have stakes in what is currently taking place

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:06.690
in Afghanistan, and especially what comes
next in Afghanistan.

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:13.850
China for some time now has been playing a
more active and direct role in promoting reconciliation

00:24:13.850 --> 00:24:19.210
talks between the Afghan government of President
Ghani and the Taliban.

00:24:19.210 --> 00:24:24.130
Unfortunately, I regret to say there's not
been much success in bringing those parties

00:24:24.130 --> 00:24:25.990
together.

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:29.850
But there's no question that the stakes are
high there.

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:34.990
And I'd like to ask our panelists to give
their thoughts on this flashpoint and where

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:35.990
it's heading.

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:40.050
And I would like to begin with Samina, and
then we'll just go down the line.

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:47.350
AHMED: As we speak, the spring offensive has
started in Afghanistan at a time when there

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:55.650
was talk about the Taliban holding direct
talks, direct negotiations, with Kabul in

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:58.900
March.

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:04.150
Quite obviously the attack, the most recent
attack in the capital, which has been the

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:14.150
worst since the international intervention,
has changed Afghan perceptions on the utility

00:25:14.150 --> 00:25:23.090
of talks, not necessarily President Ghani's,
but even the president will find it difficult

00:25:23.090 --> 00:25:37.530
now to justify direct negotiations unless
he sees the other three partners who were

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:50.890
in this process of helping bridge the gaps-the
United States, China, and Pakistan-more effectively

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:57.340
convince the Taliban into abandoning violence.

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:59.390
The stakes are way too high.

00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:02.090
The political stakes are way too high.

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:09.180
So whatever happens in the next few weeks-we're
not even talking months anymore-in Afghanistan

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:16.960
will determine two major issues-one, the relationship
and the directions of the relationship between

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:20.620
Islamabad and Kabul.

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:23.750
How much worse can it get?

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:32.970
Second-and this is equally important-is the
internal political dynamics within Kabul and

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:39.720
the tensions within a national unity government
stitched together by the United States, where

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:47.460
there are different perceptions on the utility
of negotiations.

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:54.040
So where Afghanistan stands today, I think,
is-should be a matter of concern.

00:26:54.040 --> 00:27:03.680
And quite obviously Pakistani policy and the
Afghan reaction, to a considerable extent,

00:27:03.680 --> 00:27:09.580
is going to be dictated by U.S. policy choices
in particular-and I know this is an issue

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:17.670
that's going to be talked about-whether the
U.S. troop presence is going to be reduced

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:28.710
by the end of the year or sustained, and what
the role of the other major actors is going

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:37.240
to be in dealing with the Pakistani-Afghan
relationship.

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:43.970
MARKEY: I just had a chance last week to go
to Brussels, to NATO headquarters, and participate

00:27:43.970 --> 00:27:46.840
in a series of conversations about Afghanistan.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:48.550
And it was interesting.

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:55.450
It was revealing in part because there were
some at least serious conversation about whether

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:58.720
Afghanistan would hold even through this summer.

00:27:58.720 --> 00:28:03.280
Now, I wouldn't say that that was-reflects
any kind of a consensus.

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:09.440
But the fact that that was even being raised
in quite so dire terms, I thought, was important.

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:10.440
That's my first point.

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:16.110
My second point would be, with respect to
the reconciliation dialogue, it is probably

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:18.500
at this point the right way to go.

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:22.910
It is probably the only way to go, from a
U.S. perspective, for U.S. policymakers.

00:28:22.910 --> 00:28:25.830
There aren't a lot of other great options
on the table.

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:31.429
But it has always been an unlikely thing to
work.

00:28:31.429 --> 00:28:37.480
And right now the dynamic is one in which
the United States appears, pretty much from

00:28:37.480 --> 00:28:43.770
every perspective that I can see, to be the
only party that is as desperate for a deal

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:50.210
in the short term as would be necessary to
actually see serious and rapid progress.

00:28:50.210 --> 00:28:55.570
Third point, I would want to highlight something
that Raja said about the Chinese involvement

00:28:55.570 --> 00:28:59.679
in the region and the Chinese involvement
in reconciliation dialogue.

00:28:59.679 --> 00:29:03.400
This should not be taken lightly, although
I don't see evidence that the Chinese have

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:10.710
made moves that will determine or make more
effective these talks in the near term.

00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:15.620
But the very fact that they have been involved
is unprecedented.

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:20.080
And I think it does, if we broaden out a little
bit, reflect what I think will be the new

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:26.130
reality of what I would call China's western
periphery or Western Asia, where the United

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:31.920
States will have to take into account very
seriously Chinese diplomatic moves in much

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:36.600
of what it does, whether it's in South Asia,
Central Asia, and I think increasingly, if

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:40.450
we're willing to look further afield, into
the Middle East as well.

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:42.120
China is going to have to be consulted.

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:48.200
And in some cases there will be utility in
bringing China in directly in ways that American

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:53.110
diplomats have been unfamiliar with for-well,
forever.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:54.110
So that's a big change.

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:55.960
MOHAN: Rick, I think three points.

00:29:55.960 --> 00:30:02.059
I mean, I think, one, I wouldn't be honest
if I don't share my skepticism of the flashpoint

00:30:02.059 --> 00:30:06.940
model that kind of is very common in Washington.

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:12.240
The idea somehow the U.S. is removed and there
is a dynamic in the region, and that U.S.

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:18.090
can, you know, define policy choices that
will define outcomes-you know, often it's

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:23.420
defined in public good-you know, international
terrorism norms, nonproliferation, all this

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:29.350
stuff, you know-but the fact is, the U.S.
has been in the gutter with us.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:34.200
So it's not as if U.S. is above and we're
all players moving around, and the U.S. can

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:36.140
fix solutions for that.

00:30:36.140 --> 00:30:41.830
The choices the U.S. made in the Cold War
in the late '70s vis-�-vis Russia-Soviet

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:47.750
Russia at that point-the choices it made vis-�-vis
Pakistan and Afghanistan since then, they-so

00:30:47.750 --> 00:30:50.270
they shape-they produce the reactions from
the others.

00:30:50.270 --> 00:30:53.730
So therefore, you are very much part of the
mix.

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:56.760
So it's not something you stand about and
can fix the problem.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:58.190
You're very much part of it.

00:30:58.190 --> 00:31:03.760
And your domestic politics is going to play
a big role in what happens in the-in the outcome.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:10.179
The second point, I think, is the-is the fact
that the international occupation-or shall

00:31:10.179 --> 00:31:14.460
I put it more simply, the U.S. occupation
of Afghanistan-is going to be a failure, a

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:16.090
failure writ large today.

00:31:16.090 --> 00:31:20.330
This administration would want to keep it
just standing so that when January 21st it

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:22.570
will be somebody else's problem.

00:31:22.570 --> 00:31:27.050
And if we heard this president right, who
said, look, he's not going to play by Washington's

00:31:27.050 --> 00:31:32.360
playbook in the Middle East, my sense is the
next president-one of them certainly is not

00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:35.500
going to play by Washington playbook on Afghanistan.

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:42.799
So you-so you have, actually, considerable
uncertainty in terms of what the U.S. does.

00:31:42.799 --> 00:31:44.430
That opens-I think that brings me to the third
point.

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:50.690
There is going to be significant, substantive
regional jockeying for position in Afghanistan.

00:31:50.690 --> 00:31:53.030
The Chinese are just the one part of it.

00:31:53.030 --> 00:31:58.830
Iran, I think, will play a much bigger role
than India in terms of-because of their religious-ethnic

00:31:58.830 --> 00:31:59.830
connection to Afghanistan.

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.830
They're going to play a much bigger role.

00:32:02.830 --> 00:32:08.170
But my sense is India, Iran, Saudi Arabia,
Russia, China, everyone is going to be jockeying

00:32:08.170 --> 00:32:09.170
around.

00:32:09.170 --> 00:32:12.550
Of course, the U.S. can have a lot of fun
just stepping back and watching it, but I

00:32:12.550 --> 00:32:14.190
suppose that won't be the policy line.

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:18.390
But the fact is, the agency of the regional
powers is going to grow, and I think how this

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:20.070
plays out is going to be important.

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:23.870
Lastly, the point-Pakistan is the Hamlet in
this play.

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:25.360
They are the principal players.

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:30.040
They've inherited the legacy of the British
Raj, someone who's going to extend influence

00:32:30.040 --> 00:32:32.000
on the subcontinent into Afghanistan.

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:34.500
But the tragedy is Pakistan is not the Raj.

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:36.460
It doesn't have the resources of the Raj.

00:32:36.460 --> 00:32:39.679
So it has the-its grasp-its ambition is beyond
its grasp.

00:32:39.679 --> 00:32:44.660
That is, it wants to have a special controlling
partnership in Afghanistan, but it doesn't

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:50.470
have the resources or the political vision
to construct an arrangement that would legitimize

00:32:50.470 --> 00:32:51.470
it.

00:32:51.470 --> 00:32:56.010
INDERFURTH: Dr. Wang, could you tell us a
little bit about the evolution of Chinese

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:57.760
thinking about Afghanistan?

00:32:57.760 --> 00:33:04.040
Because I dealt with China back in the 6+2
process, and China was certainly concerned

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:07.660
about Afghanistan but was not-it was basically
at arm's length.

00:33:07.660 --> 00:33:11.910
But there does seem to have been, over the
number-last number of years and last number

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:16.640
of months a greater Chinese direct involvement,
not only on the economic side and the copper

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:22.180
realm and the rest, but also in the diplomacy
and the security side.

00:33:22.180 --> 00:33:24.179
Could you talk a little bit about that?

00:33:24.179 --> 00:33:27.640
WANG: There are different arguments back in
China.

00:33:27.640 --> 00:33:36.700
So I can just speak on my own because, as
always, I'm quite skeptical always of Afghanistan,

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:38.940
the future, even at the current stage.

00:33:38.940 --> 00:33:43.620
Number one is that I'm quite skeptical about
these peace talks, because both the Kabul

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:51.030
regime cannot reach consensus about the negotiation
and how the target they will achieve; and

00:33:51.030 --> 00:34:00.799
on the other side, the Taliban, they're also-because
they needs also a reorganizing process of

00:34:00.799 --> 00:34:05.610
the Afghan Taliban nowadays, so it is also
difficult for them to reach the consensus

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:08.340
of how much they could bargain.

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:10.419
So it's hard to say about this.

00:34:10.419 --> 00:34:16.480
Even China, for example, we have the QCG,
Quadrilateral Coordination Group, and these

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:19.740
four-party dialogues; many high expectations
about that.

00:34:19.740 --> 00:34:26.041
But I'm always, always very skeptical, because
the key issue is that-at least of QCG-is that

00:34:26.041 --> 00:34:32.419
if we can't persuade the Afghan Taliban back
to the negotiation table, what the option

00:34:32.419 --> 00:34:33.750
B we could have?

00:34:33.750 --> 00:34:40.820
And what practical measures we could have
to persuade or by other means to bring the

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:42.270
Taliban back to the table?

00:34:42.270 --> 00:34:45.679
That is the key issue with the QCG nowadays.

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:49.020
Second is that I'm more concerned about not
peace talks.

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:55.300
As I always mentioned, that enhancing the
capability or stability of the Kabul regime

00:34:55.300 --> 00:35:00.859
should be on the top of the list-the list
of the top, because you see the NUG government

00:35:00.859 --> 00:35:06.190
is dual-head, and the-it is not China who
brokered the last deal.

00:35:06.190 --> 00:35:10.600
It is the United States, and with the help
of the international community made a deal.

00:35:10.600 --> 00:35:15.820
But two years has come already, and they repeatedly
postponed off the parliamentary elections

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:18.859
and the unformed loya jirga.

00:35:18.859 --> 00:35:26.990
So it will be a concern, a natural concern
about if this government could collapse, if

00:35:26.990 --> 00:35:33.010
Abdullah Abdullah and Dr. Ghani couldn't make
the further consensus of maintaining the government.

00:35:33.010 --> 00:35:37.830
The second point is that about the economy,
because people never mention-seldom mention

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:38.830
about the economy.

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:44.900
We observed last year a significant declining
of the Afghanistan GDP growth.

00:35:44.900 --> 00:35:51.800
It's negative or minus 0.5, just, and also
the high rate of unemployment, almost more

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:53.230
than 50 percent.

00:35:53.230 --> 00:36:00.080
So how could you expect, in such a circumstance,
the government could maintain stable?

00:36:00.080 --> 00:36:06.540
And how more leverage they could have on the
negotiation table if Taliban got back into

00:36:06.540 --> 00:36:07.540
the government?

00:36:07.540 --> 00:36:09.450
Sorry, to the negotiating table.

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:15.220
The third point I want to mention is that
even if, because of the rise of the IS in

00:36:15.220 --> 00:36:18.990
Afghanistan, we observe the-I almost describe
as three-three.

00:36:18.990 --> 00:36:25.380
Three is that-the first three that there's
three types of militants or the forces or

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:27.260
terrorist groups in Afghanistan.

00:36:27.260 --> 00:36:32.180
Namely, that's the Afghan Taliban, the al-Qaida,
and also the IS.

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:36.210
The second three is that they are fighting
against-for example, the Taliban is not only

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:38.160
fighting against the government.

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:41.010
The Taliban is also fighting against the IS.

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:45.750
And the Taliban, they are fighting against
themselves, like the Mullah Mansoor and Mullah

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:46.750
Rasool.

00:36:46.750 --> 00:36:47.990
So it's quite confusing.

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:52.340
And also, there are different areas which
focusing-also three areas: east part, the

00:36:52.340 --> 00:36:53.700
northern part, and the southern part.

00:36:53.700 --> 00:36:58.610
How could the government, or with the help
of the international community, along with

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:03.050
China, India, and all the regional players,
could help the Afghan government, and particularly

00:37:03.050 --> 00:37:08.470
the ANSF, to maintain a relatively stable
Afghan security situation?

00:37:08.470 --> 00:37:11.480
So that is my key point.

00:37:11.480 --> 00:37:19.190
I think, although I am quite-always very much
pessimistic about Istanbul process-in my understanding

00:37:19.190 --> 00:37:24.730
it's dying process, but still they need to
inject some (robust ?) in this.

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:31.660
So the interesting thing is that, two years
back, the third round of Istanbul process

00:37:31.660 --> 00:37:37.100
in Beijing, we initiated about the direct
peace talk between Taliban and the Kabul regime.

00:37:37.100 --> 00:37:42.650
Last year, in Islamabad, they initiate about
this Quadrilateral-QCG negotiation.

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:47.420
And this year, India will be host for the
next round of Istanbul process.

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:52.980
The significance of Istanbul process I want
to mention is that it's-already it's lack

00:37:52.980 --> 00:37:54.430
of implementation.

00:37:54.430 --> 00:38:00.460
But the problem-the question is that it is
the only regional security cooperation or

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:06.270
the cooperation mechanisms by Afghan-led and
Afghan-owned process.

00:38:06.270 --> 00:38:12.620
So the key solution for this is that, without
sitting aside and looking at its dying, but

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:19.870
let's say the further implementation of the
CBMs, and also more concentrating about, for

00:38:19.870 --> 00:38:24.340
example, the more coordination between the
supporting-leading states and the supporting

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:31.350
countries-to make more cooperation between
these two parts, to have further real implementations

00:38:31.350 --> 00:38:36.430
of the CBM without just all promising too
much.

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:37.750
Thank you.

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:41.750
INDERFURTH: A lot of what they've just said,
I hope that your questions will sort of pick

00:38:41.750 --> 00:38:48.940
up on some of these remarks, including I just
heard a reference to ISIS and its concerns

00:38:48.940 --> 00:38:50.119
about ISIS.

00:38:50.119 --> 00:38:55.780
And Samina, during the Q&amp;A hopefully you'll
address that about the degree to which ISIS

00:38:55.780 --> 00:39:03.520
is extending itself into Southern Asia-Pakistan,
Afghanistan, and the rest; also more on the

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:08.150
subject of India's concerns about the direction
that Afghanistan is heading.

00:39:08.150 --> 00:39:15.250
I want to just throw out one more subject
as a final question, which we'll try to do

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:23.830
very quickly, and that is another major flashpoint
deals with the bomb.

00:39:23.830 --> 00:39:28.150
All three countries have nuclear weapons,
lots of them.

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:34.520
You could say actually that Southern Asia
is the most nuclearized region of the world.

00:39:34.520 --> 00:39:39.270
All three countries are also modernizing their
nuclear assets.

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:44.970
It's estimated that Pakistan's nuclear arsenal
may be on track to become the world's third-largest

00:39:44.970 --> 00:39:47.990
after the U.S. and Russia.

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:51.440
So the question is, what do we make of this?

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:55.220
Clearly each state is looking over their shoulder
at the others.

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:58.880
It's called the cascading effect.

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:02.910
The question is, is there any possibility
of the three beginning a dialogue about their

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:06.180
nuclear futures?

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:13.619
And so my question is to each of the panelists
very quickly, how serious is this challenge?

00:40:13.619 --> 00:40:15.440
What do you think can be done?

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:18.210
Because clearly it's not going to go away.

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:19.210
Samina.

00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:29.710
AHMED: There are very few chances of any kind
of a dialogue between Pakistan and India on

00:40:29.710 --> 00:40:39.680
what to do about the bomb, their bombs, and
the way they perceive each other's bombs.

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:43.991
What we do see-and I completely agree with
you-this is no longer an arms crawl.

00:40:43.991 --> 00:40:47.609
This is an arms race, a nuclear arms race
in the region.

00:40:47.609 --> 00:40:50.530
It's hugely destabilizing.

00:40:50.530 --> 00:41:00.849
But if neither New Delhi nor Islamabad understand
the importance of putting some restraint on

00:41:00.849 --> 00:41:05.490
developing their nuclear arsenals and then
dealing with each other-I mean, you know,

00:41:05.490 --> 00:41:09.570
the concepts of deterrence are very odd.

00:41:09.570 --> 00:41:12.000
Let me put it this way.

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:14.670
They don't quite understand the concept.

00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:21.119
Given the fact that there's such mutual mistrust
and their ambitions, and they have nuclear

00:41:21.119 --> 00:41:31.869
weapons, and there's such short response times,
and they have disputed borders, that is, in

00:41:31.869 --> 00:41:37.610
my understanding, the major flashpoint in
South Asia.

00:41:37.610 --> 00:41:38.750
INDERFURTH: Dan.

00:41:38.750 --> 00:41:41.359
MARKEY: Yeah, I would agree with the pessimism.

00:41:41.359 --> 00:41:46.400
I see very little opportunity for a traditional
arms-control approach to the challenge of

00:41:46.400 --> 00:41:51.510
nuclear weapons in South Asia, in part because,
as you mentioned, you do have this broader

00:41:51.510 --> 00:41:55.599
dimension, which is South Asia is not limited
to South Asia.

00:41:55.599 --> 00:41:57.310
India is not looking simply at Pakistan.

00:41:57.310 --> 00:42:00.119
It's looking at China, and I think increasingly
so.

00:42:00.119 --> 00:42:06.380
And it's looking both at a nuclear China but
also a major conventionally armed China.

00:42:06.380 --> 00:42:11.240
So when India makes its calculations, it invariably
will have to make them with both China and

00:42:11.240 --> 00:42:17.960
Pakistan in mind, which leads it to build
accordingly, which then leads to Pakistani

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:19.359
insecurity.

00:42:19.359 --> 00:42:23.610
And it reinforces-and, of course, you can't
subtract us from the equation because China

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:25.120
is, of course, not just thinking about India.

00:42:25.120 --> 00:42:27.080
It's thinking principally about us.

00:42:27.080 --> 00:42:33.690
So when you play all these pieces together,
you don't get to a simple unraveling.

00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:39.340
And even if the United States were to take
a leading role in an arms-control effort,

00:42:39.340 --> 00:42:45.100
you come back to the fundamental insecurities
particularly felt by Pakistan and its sense

00:42:45.100 --> 00:42:53.970
that conventional plus sort of the asymmetrical
use of militant proxies is still not sufficient.

00:42:53.970 --> 00:42:59.160
And its leaders believe that the nuclear dimension
is absolutely essential and that continuing

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:01.780
to invest in that is necessary.

00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:08.990
Some efforts by local think tanks to convince
Pakistanis otherwise, to suggest that enough

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:14.109
is enough, that they have a minimal credible
deterrent, have fallen on completely deaf

00:43:14.109 --> 00:43:15.109
ears.

00:43:15.109 --> 00:43:18.170
And I gather that similar approaches by the
U.S. government have as well.

00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:23.040
So the moment is, as they say, not ripe for
this conversation, even though the concerns

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:24.040
are very real.

00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:26.330
MOHAN: Rick, three quick notes.

00:43:26.330 --> 00:43:31.099
One, I don't think this is such a big deal
as the Americans make it out to be, the nuclear

00:43:31.099 --> 00:43:32.940
flashpoint in South Asia.

00:43:32.940 --> 00:43:38.680
If you see what's going on in East Asia right
now, you have a presidential candidate who's

00:43:38.680 --> 00:43:43.590
saying if the Japanese don't want to pay for
it, they're on their own; and if they're on

00:43:43.590 --> 00:43:45.800
their own, I don't mind their getting nuclear
weapons.

00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:52.600
Now, you're talking about a scale of a transformation
in Asia which is going to be far more consequential

00:43:52.600 --> 00:43:56.130
and powerful than the flashpoint argument
of South Asia.

00:43:56.130 --> 00:43:59.990
We've kind of been at it for the last three
decades or more.

00:43:59.990 --> 00:44:04.530
We've been-all of us have been part of that
debate, which has actually not moved an inch

00:44:04.530 --> 00:44:07.420
since we started the debate, since '87.

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:13.020
And second part is it is-one is what happens
to U.S. extended deterrence in East Asia is

00:44:13.020 --> 00:44:14.020
going to be a big issue.

00:44:14.020 --> 00:44:16.810
That's going to come to haunt us in the next
coming years.

00:44:16.810 --> 00:44:21.940
And second, the question of what's happening
between U.S. and China?

00:44:21.940 --> 00:44:26.619
I mean, what happens between India and Pakistan
is a picnic if we see-if the doctrine of the

00:44:26.619 --> 00:44:31.390
Third Offset strategy the Americans are talking
about, the Chinese say we're not going to

00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:36.680
let the Americans operate too close to our
shores, you're talking about a tension out

00:44:36.680 --> 00:44:41.530
there between American forward military presence,
the role of nuclear weapons and extended deterrence,

00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:45.620
and the capacity of the Chinese to push the
Americans further away.

00:44:45.620 --> 00:44:47.250
That is the flashpoint.

00:44:47.250 --> 00:44:51.570
But that's a flashpoint nobody wants to talk,
because it's a work in progress.

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:56.171
I'm sure it's a matter of time that hopefully,
within six months with the new president here,

00:44:56.171 --> 00:45:00.220
a lot of that is going to come (here ?). So
I would say we have to wrack our brains with

00:45:00.220 --> 00:45:01.220
that.

00:45:01.220 --> 00:45:05.630
The second: In the subcontinent, the problem
has not been with the nuclear weapons, but

00:45:05.630 --> 00:45:09.710
the imaginative use to which Pakistan has
supported.

00:45:09.710 --> 00:45:14.790
I must give credit to our Pakistani friends
where it is due, that Pakistanis did not think

00:45:14.790 --> 00:45:21.550
like other nuclear powers-that the nuclear
umbrella provided Pakistan with the leverage

00:45:21.550 --> 00:45:24.609
to put India on the defensive.

00:45:24.609 --> 00:45:26.359
That is the purpose of Pakistani nuclear weapons.

00:45:26.359 --> 00:45:31.170
And I think it didn't matter whether they
had a thousand or 5,000 or 10,000 or just

00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:36.240
one, because Amritsar and Lahore are barely
40 kilometers away from each other.

00:45:36.240 --> 00:45:40.230
This idea that it is the structure of the
nuclear arsenal that creates the problem in

00:45:40.230 --> 00:45:46.230
subcontinent, which Washington is obsessed,
the entire think-tank community here-the problem

00:45:46.230 --> 00:45:54.700
is this: I mean, that Pakistan is not building
this arsenal to-it has the capacity to put

00:45:54.700 --> 00:45:55.740
India on the defensive.

00:45:55.740 --> 00:46:00.640
And that using sub-conventional conflict under
the rubric of nuclear deterrence, that is

00:46:00.640 --> 00:46:04.030
the genius, evil or otherwise.

00:46:04.030 --> 00:46:05.030
That is the difference.

00:46:05.030 --> 00:46:09.400
And I think, till we address that question-how
those sub-conventional conflict or terrorism

00:46:09.400 --> 00:46:13.280
fit into this equation, which Americans and
Russians never thought about it-but today,

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:16.130
in this context, how that plays out, that
is the key issue.

00:46:16.130 --> 00:46:21.010
And I think that problem is not going to be
solved by merely talking, getting the laundry

00:46:21.010 --> 00:46:27.560
list out of the conventional wisdom and then
trying to impose it on South Asia.

00:46:27.560 --> 00:46:28.560
It's not worked.

00:46:28.560 --> 00:46:32.921
And we figured out, both Indians and Pakistanis,
how to manipulate Washington, because every

00:46:32.921 --> 00:46:37.390
time Pakistanis come here and say, hey, look,
our problem, it's the Indians' limited war.

00:46:37.390 --> 00:46:40.960
I mean, you ask the Indian military, we're
nowhere near it, but that gives an excuse

00:46:40.960 --> 00:46:41.960
to the Pakistanis.

00:46:41.960 --> 00:46:44.170
And then everybody here jumps up and down.

00:46:44.170 --> 00:46:47.820
So the problem is not the nature of the nuclear
arsenal.

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:50.730
The problem is the sub-conventional conflict.

00:46:50.730 --> 00:46:53.050
And Pakistanis use it to get attention here.

00:46:53.050 --> 00:46:57.701
We have sometimes used it to blackmail the
Americans, too, I mean, which is in the-in

00:46:57.701 --> 00:47:00.580
the post-Cargill period in 2001.

00:47:00.580 --> 00:47:02.940
If we don't do this, are we going to do something?

00:47:02.940 --> 00:47:04.810
So that's now-we know how to play Washington.

00:47:04.810 --> 00:47:09.060
So you can keep doing what you want-(laughter)-but
I think we know how to manage this.

00:47:09.060 --> 00:47:10.720
INDERFURTH: Dr. Wang?

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:17.020
WANG: In very short, I don't believe that
in current circumstance could China, Pakistan,

00:47:17.020 --> 00:47:23.619
and the Indian state together in harmony a
way for constructive or productive negotiation

00:47:23.619 --> 00:47:25.080
about the nuclear issues.

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:32.700
Particularly, one, you have the civil nuclear
deal with India, and also your rebalancing

00:47:32.700 --> 00:47:35.630
policies towards the Asia-Pacific.

00:47:35.630 --> 00:47:40.530
So there will be definitely more complicated
issues between these three countries.

00:47:40.530 --> 00:47:49.360
Secondly is that I believe that the stable
South Asia is in line of our national interest.

00:47:49.360 --> 00:47:54.460
I don't believe that our government could
have that much thought that maybe we could

00:47:54.460 --> 00:47:57.400
use this as leverage towards either India
or Pakistan.

00:47:57.400 --> 00:47:59.299
No, I don't believe so.

00:47:59.299 --> 00:48:03.670
The third point is that that is why we are
focusing on (socioeconomic ?) cooperations.

00:48:03.670 --> 00:48:07.670
We are more focusing on that.

00:48:07.670 --> 00:48:11.900
So, on the basis of this, I think on nuclear
issues we could have some sort of bilateral

00:48:11.900 --> 00:48:12.900
dialogues.

00:48:12.900 --> 00:48:17.140
(Let's say ? ) we could have the civil nuclear
cooperation with Pakistan.

00:48:17.140 --> 00:48:23.490
At the same time, we also extend our cooperation
and hands to the Indian side when your prime

00:48:23.490 --> 00:48:25.380
minister visited China last year.

00:48:25.380 --> 00:48:26.710
MOHAN: A civilian nuclear deal as well.

00:48:26.710 --> 00:48:27.710
WANG: Yes.

00:48:27.710 --> 00:48:28.710
Yeah, yeah, about this.

00:48:28.710 --> 00:48:35.710
So I think about the future on this could
be-we could have another thought, at least,

00:48:35.710 --> 00:48:37.490
on these civil nuclear issues.

00:48:37.490 --> 00:48:38.930
INDERFURTH: Good.

00:48:38.930 --> 00:48:39.930
OK.

00:48:39.930 --> 00:48:47.130
I think it's time now to turn to members for
your questions and joining in the conversation.

00:48:47.130 --> 00:48:48.800
I think that a lot has been said here.

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:53.770
I hope that you pick up and pursue some of
these as the follow-up questions.

00:48:53.770 --> 00:48:57.930
Let me reiterate that this meeting is on the
record.

00:48:57.930 --> 00:49:01.990
Also we have microphones located around the
room.

00:49:01.990 --> 00:49:04.099
So please wait for a microphone.

00:49:04.099 --> 00:49:06.359
State your name and affiliation.

00:49:06.359 --> 00:49:12.670
Finally, please limit yourself to a brief
comment if you want to make one, and only

00:49:12.670 --> 00:49:14.170
one question.

00:49:14.170 --> 00:49:16.950
And that does not mean one question with multiple
parts.

00:49:16.950 --> 00:49:17.950
(Laughter.)

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:19.923
I've been accused of doing that myself.

00:49:19.923 --> 00:49:20.923
So let's begin.

00:49:20.923 --> 00:49:22.359
Who would like to start?

00:49:22.359 --> 00:49:27.980
I think we have a person that might have something
to say about Afghanistan back here, Barney

00:49:27.980 --> 00:49:28.980
Rubin.

00:49:28.980 --> 00:49:29.980
Q: I do, but I'll say it later.

00:49:29.980 --> 00:49:31.240
This is about something else.

00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:35.980
Barney Rubin, Center on International Cooperation.

00:49:35.980 --> 00:49:45.260
In all my discussions with Chinese about South
Asia and the possibility of U.S.-China cooperation

00:49:45.260 --> 00:49:51.960
over South Asia, I have found that the single
issue on which we have the greatest disagreement

00:49:51.960 --> 00:49:59.089
and the greatest divergence of perception
is Pakistan's nuclear weapons in that the

00:49:59.089 --> 00:50:06.340
United States is and has been concerned for
quite some time about not nuclear weapons

00:50:06.340 --> 00:50:10.400
per se, but the type of nuclear weapons, the
turn toward conventional weapons, the doctrine

00:50:10.400 --> 00:50:18.670
of first use as a deterrent to conventional
forces, and-whereas Pakistan, which says that

00:50:18.670 --> 00:50:22.880
stability of South Asia is so important to
it, has never shared those-China, rather-has

00:50:22.880 --> 00:50:27.640
never-it says stability is so important to
it-does not appear to share those concerns.

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:32.370
So I want to start with Professor Wang to
comment on that.

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:39.340
WANG: My friend Barney has raised a very tough
question about this.

00:50:39.340 --> 00:50:42.440
I will try my best.

00:50:42.440 --> 00:50:50.849
According to my humble understanding, Pakistan
use nuclear asset as a deterrence measures.

00:50:50.849 --> 00:50:53.512
I don't believe that Pakistan will ever use
this.

00:50:53.512 --> 00:50:57.760
Well, it depends.

00:50:57.760 --> 00:51:05.400
Second point is that you are seeing China's
influence towards Pakistan about these issues.

00:51:05.400 --> 00:51:12.500
But I would like to raise another issue that,
for example, a few days back we met our common

00:51:12.500 --> 00:51:14.360
Afghan friend, old friends.

00:51:14.360 --> 00:51:23.630
And he also raised another issue about how
China asked Pakistan for this QCG mechanism

00:51:23.630 --> 00:51:24.859
more effectively.

00:51:24.859 --> 00:51:35.860
The question is that, how could you-how could
you ask the-raise the issues which they do

00:51:35.860 --> 00:51:39.990
not willing to negotiate with your side?

00:51:39.990 --> 00:51:49.490
Second is that I think I'm more concerning
about not in the conventional warfightings

00:51:49.490 --> 00:51:56.550
using the nuclear, but I'm personally quite
concerned about the terrorist threat to the

00:51:56.550 --> 00:51:58.589
nuclear asset in Pakistan.

00:51:58.589 --> 00:52:03.630
But, at the same time, the Pakistan government
always promised that we have full protection

00:52:03.630 --> 00:52:07.520
of that, and we fully believe that the Pakistan
government and the military could have the

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:09.560
capability of doing so.

00:52:09.560 --> 00:52:14.910
The question is that, how to prevent from
the arm race in the region?

00:52:14.910 --> 00:52:22.050
Then I would say something my Indian friend
might not-(chuckles)-pleased to listen.

00:52:22.050 --> 00:52:26.510
One is they could have less pressures from
the eastern border or the western border.

00:52:26.510 --> 00:52:32.220
Then Pakistan could have another thought,
or the arguments-even inside Pakistan if they

00:52:32.220 --> 00:52:36.440
have different arguments about the nuclear
policies or strategies.

00:52:36.440 --> 00:52:41.501
But if they are still facing very strong pressures
from both east side and the west side, so

00:52:41.501 --> 00:52:48.190
how could they have very natural logic thinking
on their nuclear strategy?

00:52:48.190 --> 00:52:50.630
MOHAN: Just two things.

00:52:50.630 --> 00:52:55.180
I mean, I think the arms-race idea, again,
I think it's, again, an overused metaphor.

00:52:55.180 --> 00:52:59.530
I mean, you see what-China did not follow
the U.S. or the Russians.

00:52:59.530 --> 00:53:04.760
I mean, after 40 years of having-50 years
of having nuclear weapons, they're still at

00:53:04.760 --> 00:53:05.760
400-plus.

00:53:05.760 --> 00:53:08.190
That's not a race with anyone.

00:53:08.190 --> 00:53:12.340
India is a-is a lazy slob, I mean, that we're
not even reprocessing the material that we

00:53:12.340 --> 00:53:15.380
have lying around-you know, civilian things,
which are unsafeguarded.

00:53:15.380 --> 00:53:19.210
So it's not-if anybody says, look, India is
crawling still, is not racing with anyone.

00:53:19.210 --> 00:53:21.930
Pakistan, yes, has built a large arsenal.

00:53:21.930 --> 00:53:25.510
Much of it, I believe, is directed to the
fears that the U.S. might do something about

00:53:25.510 --> 00:53:26.510
it.

00:53:26.510 --> 00:53:31.170
Because when the U.S. talks about, you know,
preemptive, other stuff, that it's a response

00:53:31.170 --> 00:53:36.869
to having such kind of actions rather than
fearing a threat from India.

00:53:36.869 --> 00:53:41.109
But as far as the east front being volatile,
I thought it was the Americans who were dropping

00:53:41.109 --> 00:53:42.359
drones on the western frontier.

00:53:42.359 --> 00:53:47.050
I mean, we haven't attacked Pakistan by any
stretch of the imagination.

00:53:47.050 --> 00:53:51.630
Ever since Pakistan has had nuclear weapons,
the eastern front has been relatively quiet.

00:53:51.630 --> 00:53:55.140
In fact, any questions are from the other
side, though some of us might want to do it

00:53:55.140 --> 00:53:56.140
other way.

00:53:56.140 --> 00:53:57.900
But the fact is, which frontier has been volatile?

00:53:57.900 --> 00:54:02.369
I mean, it is the Americans who drop drones
on Pakistan's western frontiers.

00:54:02.369 --> 00:54:03.369
We don't.

00:54:03.369 --> 00:54:08.440
So I think this idea that somehow the eastern
front is what drives Pakistan-I mean, I think

00:54:08.440 --> 00:54:14.430
the last 20 years, 25 years, 30 years, that
India has had no option even to retaliate

00:54:14.430 --> 00:54:16.310
against Pakistan terror.

00:54:16.310 --> 00:54:21.720
To say Pakistan faces a threat-I mean, I think,
look, at some point both the U.S. and China

00:54:21.720 --> 00:54:27.510
will have to start asking basic questions
and not repeat the same mantra that you (conveniently

00:54:27.510 --> 00:54:29.540
think ?) that people have said for themselves.

00:54:29.540 --> 00:54:30.560
INDERFURTH: Dan, quick comment.

00:54:30.560 --> 00:54:31.560
MARKEY: Yeah, just-
INDERFURTH: Then we'll turn to another question.

00:54:31.560 --> 00:54:36.790
MARKEY: Just to pick up on this last point,
the situation, yes, between India and Pakistan

00:54:36.790 --> 00:54:41.210
has certain aspects that have been the same
for now a couple of decades.

00:54:41.210 --> 00:54:45.890
And Raja's right to see some reason to expect
that to persist.

00:54:45.890 --> 00:54:49.329
But there are two dynamic elements that have
come up already.

00:54:49.329 --> 00:54:55.950
One is China's evolving role with Pakistan
and China's increasing focus on Pakistan and

00:54:55.950 --> 00:55:02.930
involvement in Pakistan, which I think and
I'm hopeful will make China-Chinese scholars,

00:55:02.930 --> 00:55:07.430
Chinese government officials-increasingly
concerned about the development that Pakistan's

00:55:07.430 --> 00:55:09.410
nuclear-weapons program is taking.

00:55:09.410 --> 00:55:10.810
And that's the other big development.

00:55:10.810 --> 00:55:16.440
It's not the change, rapid change in India's
nuclear arsenal that gets people concerned.

00:55:16.440 --> 00:55:19.140
It's the rapid development of Pakistan's nuclear
arsenal.

00:55:19.140 --> 00:55:24.670
So the two dynamic elements of this broader
equation are right there-China's involvement,

00:55:24.670 --> 00:55:25.750
Pakistan's development.

00:55:25.750 --> 00:55:29.260
Those two things together have the potential
to continue to be of great concern.

00:55:29.260 --> 00:55:33.960
Or we might begin to see the Chinese take
a different approach, take a different view

00:55:33.960 --> 00:55:35.359
toward Pakistan's program.

00:55:35.359 --> 00:55:39.550
Now, whether they would have then the leverage
to do anything about it, that's a separate

00:55:39.550 --> 00:55:40.550
issue.

00:55:40.550 --> 00:55:42.450
INDERFURTH: Questions?

00:55:42.450 --> 00:55:43.450
Please.

00:55:43.450 --> 00:55:49.420
There's one right-second table from the back.

00:55:49.420 --> 00:55:50.420
Q: Hi.

00:55:50.420 --> 00:55:52.099
Good morning, and thank you so much for the
interesting conversation.

00:55:52.099 --> 00:55:55.660
My name is Louis Bergeron from Booz Allen
Hamilton, which is the company that coined

00:55:55.660 --> 00:56:01.329
the term String of Pearls back in the mid-2000s,
talking about Chinese energy routes and the

00:56:01.329 --> 00:56:02.329
Indian Ocean.

00:56:02.329 --> 00:56:05.220
I'm just wondering if there's an update on
the string of pearls.

00:56:05.220 --> 00:56:11.540
We've heard a lot about developments in Djibouti
for the Chinese PLA, the People's Liberation

00:56:11.540 --> 00:56:18.510
Navy-Army, or Army-Navy, in Djibouti, and
just wondering about India's developments

00:56:18.510 --> 00:56:23.790
in maybe East Africa, the Middle East, and
other bases in the Indian Ocean region, and

00:56:23.790 --> 00:56:29.359
also Chinese developments in that Indian Ocean
region from East Africa through Southeast

00:56:29.359 --> 00:56:30.359
Asia.

00:56:30.359 --> 00:56:32.650
INDERFURTH: Let's do discuss that.

00:56:32.650 --> 00:56:34.460
I was hoping that would come up.

00:56:34.460 --> 00:56:40.680
I have heard a great expression that the Chinese
are more interested in places, not bases,

00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:42.550
as their current strategy.

00:56:42.550 --> 00:56:43.550
India may be as well.

00:56:43.550 --> 00:56:49.060
So panelists, who would like to start?

00:56:49.060 --> 00:56:54.540
WANG: I think this question should be already
discussed.

00:56:54.540 --> 00:57:00.109
As I mentioned, the government-for example,
there is a huge lesson that we learned from

00:57:00.109 --> 00:57:01.640
the Libya issues.

00:57:01.640 --> 00:57:08.520
One, there something happened, and we want
to withdraw our labors and our projects-(inaudible).

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:12.109
But we have a very limited capability of withdrawal.

00:57:12.109 --> 00:57:14.950
And in the end the government was under huge
pressure.

00:57:14.950 --> 00:57:17.680
We have a huge public media.

00:57:17.680 --> 00:57:23.490
And the government was blamed for lack of
responsibility and the lack of the capability

00:57:23.490 --> 00:57:25.060
of doing so.

00:57:25.060 --> 00:57:34.119
And then we have some military existence in
Indian Ocean, in Djibouti, or some-in Aden

00:57:34.119 --> 00:57:35.119
Gulf.

00:57:35.119 --> 00:57:45.440
Then we have the experience of Yemen issues,
and we could send our warships to withdraw

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:46.440
our workers.

00:57:46.440 --> 00:57:52.180
And at the same time, we also withdraw some
other international foreigners who worked

00:57:52.180 --> 00:57:53.180
in Yemen.

00:57:53.180 --> 00:58:01.650
So that is a vivid example of how China's
rational logic of military existence or presence

00:58:01.650 --> 00:58:03.579
in the different areas.

00:58:03.579 --> 00:58:10.500
Second is that how effective-how constructive
a role China's military existence could play

00:58:10.500 --> 00:58:12.470
for world peace.

00:58:12.470 --> 00:58:16.339
MOHAN: I think Chinese have moved beyond a
places-and-bases debate.

00:58:16.339 --> 00:58:20.859
I mean, I think have explicitly stated they're
building a base in Djibouti.

00:58:20.859 --> 00:58:24.360
And Djibouti has discovered the virtues of
nonalignment.

00:58:24.360 --> 00:58:26.160
They've offered it to you.

00:58:26.160 --> 00:58:28.589
They've offered it to the Chinese.

00:58:28.589 --> 00:58:31.210
The Japanese have a little base as well.

00:58:31.210 --> 00:58:33.820
If you pay the right amount, I'm sure we'll
get one as well.

00:58:33.820 --> 00:58:39.950
So there is, I think, everybody, every little
speck, every little island in the Indian Ocean

00:58:39.950 --> 00:58:45.500
today sees the opportunity that, look, China
is emerging as a power; China will come to

00:58:45.500 --> 00:58:47.030
the Indian Ocean.

00:58:47.030 --> 00:58:49.120
So I don't think-there's no point grudging
that.

00:58:49.120 --> 00:58:55.130
I mean, I think Julie Manderno (sp), the Booz
Hamilton-who did the report, I think, saw

00:58:55.130 --> 00:58:56.130
what was coming.

00:58:56.130 --> 00:58:57.130
And I think it's inevitable.

00:58:57.130 --> 00:58:58.369
It's a natural occurrence.

00:58:58.369 --> 00:59:01.990
So I think we have to deal with that reality,
and I think India, too.

00:59:01.990 --> 00:59:06.161
I'm not-I don't represent the government,
but I think the Indian navy and the Indian

00:59:06.161 --> 00:59:10.890
government should be looking for exactly the
same thing, which is bases, facilities.

00:59:10.890 --> 00:59:15.570
They already have some turnaround arrangements,
I think, with some countries.

00:59:15.570 --> 00:59:19.910
And I think we need to do a lot more, because
if you want to project power, you've got to

00:59:19.910 --> 00:59:21.320
have bases.

00:59:21.320 --> 00:59:23.020
I mean, I think it's as simple as that.

00:59:23.020 --> 00:59:24.400
You can't fax a navy.

00:59:24.400 --> 00:59:29.180
So, therefore, whatever our rhetoric of nonalignment,
blah, blah, blah, for 50 years has been, today

00:59:29.180 --> 00:59:33.109
as rising powers, we're going to do what other
great powers did.

00:59:33.109 --> 00:59:35.150
So the question is, managing that is the issue.

00:59:35.150 --> 00:59:37.349
And there I think some interesting things.

00:59:37.349 --> 00:59:40.710
I mean, if you noticed, we haven't said a
word about Diego Garcia for how many years?

00:59:40.710 --> 00:59:41.710
I mean, maybe 20 years.

00:59:41.710 --> 00:59:44.290
We don't talk about Americans should get out
of the Indian Ocean.

00:59:44.290 --> 00:59:47.770
Maybe you guys are supposed to leave at the
end of this year, so maybe there's other arrangements

00:59:47.770 --> 00:59:49.220
we can make with each other.

00:59:49.220 --> 00:59:54.010
So there are going to be a huge number of
possibilities, from Djibouti to India to Madagascar

00:59:54.010 --> 00:59:56.860
to Mauritius to Sri Lanka.

00:59:56.860 --> 00:59:57.990
Everyone is playing this game.

00:59:57.990 --> 01:00:02.500
So it is going to be-bases is going to be
the name of the game in the Indian Ocean,

01:00:02.500 --> 01:00:06.380
and that game is going to be pretty active
in the coming years.

01:00:06.380 --> 01:00:07.630
INDERFURTH: Questions?

01:00:07.630 --> 01:00:08.640
We have two in the back.

01:00:08.640 --> 01:00:11.950
This gentleman's in the center and then to
the right.

01:00:11.950 --> 01:00:14.680
Q: Andrew Small from the German Marshall Fund.

01:00:14.680 --> 01:00:18.250
Another question to Wang Xu to kick off with.

01:00:18.250 --> 01:00:24.630
I wondered if you might elaborate a little
bit on how China sees these plan B options

01:00:24.630 --> 01:00:28.450
of peace negotiations in Afghanistan fail.

01:00:28.450 --> 01:00:34.730
What role specifically does China see itself
playing if peace negotiations don't move ahead?

01:00:34.730 --> 01:00:41.070
And specifically within that, we saw the visit
of Fang Fenghui, the chief of-PLA chief of

01:00:41.070 --> 01:00:43.820
general staff to Kabul.

01:00:43.820 --> 01:00:48.670
China's been very reluctant traditionally
in the past to expand its direct security

01:00:48.670 --> 01:00:50.349
and military cooperation with Afghanistan.

01:00:50.349 --> 01:00:57.970
What sort of scope for that do you now see,
particularly in these kind of plan B scenarios?

01:00:57.970 --> 01:01:05.970
WANG: The reason why I'm watching my watch
is that I'm watching what date is today, because

01:01:05.970 --> 01:01:14.140
according to the last round of the QCG, the
formal direct peace talks should be launched

01:01:14.140 --> 01:01:17.040
in early March.

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:23.690
And actually, if you have observed that the
MOU signed by that QCG or the announcement

01:01:23.690 --> 01:01:29.450
on that, they have the plan B or they have
option B. Option B is a general principle,

01:01:29.450 --> 01:01:35.920
which means take all necessary means to bring
the Taliban back to the-so, I mean, after

01:01:35.920 --> 01:01:43.970
almost one-half month, the four parties could
not find possible, practical measures to deal

01:01:43.970 --> 01:01:44.980
with this.

01:01:44.980 --> 01:01:48.530
So that is the reason why I raised this question.

01:01:48.530 --> 01:01:53.619
But I'm looking from another issue, is that,
as you mentioned, about the military existence

01:01:53.619 --> 01:01:57.400
or the military engagement of China towards
Afghanistan.

01:01:57.400 --> 01:01:59.710
I think it's not a sudden change.

01:01:59.710 --> 01:02:07.300
Actually, if you notice that the public media
from two years back we have sent some equipments

01:02:07.300 --> 01:02:13.070
for the Afghan police and also Afghan army,
different range of that.

01:02:13.070 --> 01:02:17.160
It's a natural concern for our side.

01:02:17.160 --> 01:02:23.710
As I always mention, Afghanistan is very important
for us, for our regional stability, and also

01:02:23.710 --> 01:02:28.760
for the west part of China-and also, more
importantly, now for the Belt and Road initiative,

01:02:28.760 --> 01:02:33.970
because we have the Silk Road initiative crossing
Central Asia.

01:02:33.970 --> 01:02:39.099
So the overflowing of the security threat
from Afghanistan could be a huge threat for

01:02:39.099 --> 01:02:44.430
the energy supplies, from our road transportation
of the energy supply for us.

01:02:44.430 --> 01:02:52.579
So the question is that whether we have enough
capability for the military engaging in Afghanistan.

01:02:52.579 --> 01:02:57.040
That is quite arguing nowadays back in China.

01:02:57.040 --> 01:03:05.060
And second thought is that, whether by military
or by the force means, can you solve the Afghan

01:03:05.060 --> 01:03:06.859
issues or not?

01:03:06.859 --> 01:03:13.840
So I personally believe that only by political
means we can solve Afghan issues at last.

01:03:13.840 --> 01:03:21.370
But, during the process of this, instead of
rather more focusing on peace talks-which,

01:03:21.370 --> 01:03:28.280
I mean, I personally believe that it's less
hopeful in short term-why not we more focusing

01:03:28.280 --> 01:03:34.130
on concentrating the Afghan government so
that you could have a more strong leverage

01:03:34.130 --> 01:03:36.869
or strong position in the negotiating table?

01:03:36.869 --> 01:03:43.369
Second is that, when we talk about the IS
issues, if the international community-because

01:03:43.369 --> 01:03:48.980
we saw a strong hesitation of the international
community coming back again in Afghanistan

01:03:48.980 --> 01:03:51.410
to fighting against the IS.

01:03:51.410 --> 01:03:55.670
The problem with IS, the influence, is that
at their current stage, the real existence

01:03:55.670 --> 01:04:03.440
of IS members mostly coming from the so-called-the
local, regional, or extremist and terrorist

01:04:03.440 --> 01:04:05.190
rebranding of that.

01:04:05.190 --> 01:04:11.540
But the threat is that if we cannot defeat
them at the very initial stage, then the peace

01:04:11.540 --> 01:04:17.230
talk between Afghan Taliban with the Kabul
regime would be maybe just a paper work, because

01:04:17.230 --> 01:04:23.170
after that part of the Taliban would go to
another side for fighting under the flag of

01:04:23.170 --> 01:04:26.990
the IS, so there will be another round of
Afghan civil war.

01:04:26.990 --> 01:04:29.650
So what is the meaning of the current peace
talk?

01:04:29.650 --> 01:04:36.200
So that is the key issue that, instead of
focusing on this less-meaningful peace talk,

01:04:36.200 --> 01:04:42.890
we should be focusing-more focusing on economic
reform and political supporting to the Kabul

01:04:42.890 --> 01:04:43.890
government.

01:04:43.890 --> 01:04:44.890
That is my understanding.

01:04:44.890 --> 01:04:49.819
INDERFURTH: Samina, do you want to mention
something on the question of ISIS and plan

01:04:49.819 --> 01:04:51.280
B, Afghanistan and the rest?

01:04:51.280 --> 01:05:03.940
AHMED: I think it is so overrated, this notion
that the Islamic State is finding conducive

01:05:03.940 --> 01:05:10.069
territory and expansion, influence in Afghanistan.

01:05:10.069 --> 01:05:13.650
It's not a matter of maybe they will in the
future.

01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:19.589
It's a matter of who are the major players
in Afghanistan?

01:05:19.589 --> 01:05:22.900
The major player in Afghanistan is the Taliban.

01:05:22.900 --> 01:05:24.760
It is not IS.

01:05:24.760 --> 01:05:30.140
The infighting between the Taliban, I think
that's overrated as well, because, yes, there

01:05:30.140 --> 01:05:32.930
is factionalism.

01:05:32.930 --> 01:05:40.839
But the present leader, Mullah Mansoor, has
fairly, I think, quickly consolidated his

01:05:40.839 --> 01:05:41.839
authority.

01:05:41.839 --> 01:05:48.170
And that's largely because of the success
of the military campaign.

01:05:48.170 --> 01:05:56.140
IS is mentioned more, in fact, by Afghan politicians
who are absolutely desperate that the international

01:05:56.140 --> 01:05:57.800
community remains engaged.

01:05:57.800 --> 01:06:02.760
And that's the one thing that captures their
imagination.

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:04.720
And does it matter?

01:06:04.720 --> 01:06:13.950
I think we tend to forget that al-Qaida has
a presence still in Pakistan, in Afghanistan.

01:06:13.950 --> 01:06:23.609
Al-Qaida in the Indian Subcontinent is now,
in a minor way, but still; a presence in Bangladesh,

01:06:23.609 --> 01:06:26.190
in the jihadi landscape.

01:06:26.190 --> 01:06:38.010
That is a far more rooted group because it
has links with local extremists, and longstanding

01:06:38.010 --> 01:06:39.010
links.

01:06:39.010 --> 01:06:43.300
So the less we talk about IS, I think the
more realistic we get about Afghanistan and

01:06:43.300 --> 01:06:46.190
what needs to be done there.

01:06:46.190 --> 01:06:49.130
Will the Taliban talk?

01:06:49.130 --> 01:06:51.900
And here is where I completely agree with
you.

01:06:51.900 --> 01:06:58.990
I think the focus there should not be necessarily
on can we this year or in the next few months

01:06:58.990 --> 01:07:07.690
then once again make these promises of either
Pakistan or the QCG convincing, cajoling the

01:07:07.690 --> 01:07:09.890
Taliban to come to the negotiating table?

01:07:09.890 --> 01:07:15.010
It's far more important to look at the issue
of political stabilization.

01:07:15.010 --> 01:07:26.090
If that doesn't-if that factor is taken out
of play, what kind of a reconciliation process

01:07:26.090 --> 01:07:28.109
can you have in the first place?

01:07:28.109 --> 01:07:36.569
There are any number of issues at stake here,
including the future of the national unity

01:07:36.569 --> 01:07:37.569
government.

01:07:37.569 --> 01:07:41.359
I mean, I think the U.S. has made it amply
clear that, no, we weren't talking about two

01:07:41.359 --> 01:07:43.140
years actually.

01:07:43.140 --> 01:07:50.400
And to be realistic, how could you have elections
this year, which is what was supposed to happen?

01:07:50.400 --> 01:07:57.190
Can you have elections next year in Afghanistan,
given the challenges that Kabul faces?

01:07:57.190 --> 01:08:01.920
But what you can have is a dialogue, and a
meaningful dialogue, between all the major

01:08:01.920 --> 01:08:10.930
Afghan actors on reform, whether-and whether
it is political reform, electoral reform is

01:08:10.930 --> 01:08:20.350
a very central part of it; whether it is economic
reform and what needs to be done as the war

01:08:20.350 --> 01:08:28.949
economy disappears and, you know, resources
become scarce.

01:08:28.949 --> 01:08:33.170
President Ghani took over power promising
reform.

01:08:33.170 --> 01:08:34.580
That was his agenda.

01:08:34.580 --> 01:08:37.489
And that's what he needs to be focusing on.

01:08:37.489 --> 01:08:42.999
INDERFURTH: This final question, so we will
keep to our timing.

01:08:42.999 --> 01:08:45.509
Q: Good morning.

01:08:45.509 --> 01:08:46.779
I'm from Djibouti embassy.

01:08:46.779 --> 01:08:47.779
I'm Mr. Jama (sp).

01:08:47.779 --> 01:08:49.439
And talking about Djibouti, I should intervene.

01:08:49.439 --> 01:08:50.439
(Laughter.)

01:08:50.439 --> 01:08:51.969
I'm sorry for my poor English.

01:08:51.969 --> 01:08:56.289
English is not my first language, but I will
try.

01:08:56.289 --> 01:09:03.729
And, first of all, I would like to remind
that Djibouti always work with the international

01:09:03.729 --> 01:09:09.029
community when crisis happen, locally or internationally.

01:09:09.029 --> 01:09:17.229
And when, after 9/11, the U.S. come to Djibouti
to ask to settle the base, we accept.

01:09:17.229 --> 01:09:21.900
Now today we have NATO also.

01:09:21.900 --> 01:09:28.349
We have Japanese when the fight against piracy
start.

01:09:28.349 --> 01:09:35.429
And we have also now the Chinese will come.

01:09:35.429 --> 01:09:39.489
And every country have their own interest
coming to Djibouti.

01:09:39.489 --> 01:09:41.299
Djibouti never asked for that.

01:09:41.299 --> 01:09:43.699
And Djibouti have its own interests, too.

01:09:43.699 --> 01:09:49.659
And the second point I would like to remind
is, before coming to Djibouti, China invest

01:09:49.659 --> 01:09:57.400
a lot in the economy, in the infrastructure,
either in East Africa-I'm talking about Djibouti,

01:09:57.400 --> 01:10:03.989
Ethiopia, Kenya, Mozambique-all these Oriental
coast.

01:10:03.989 --> 01:10:10.449
And my question is to the Professor Mohan.

01:10:10.449 --> 01:10:23.300
How India see this growing presence of China
economically, and now military?

01:10:23.300 --> 01:10:28.250
And that-and what is the response of the Indian
government?

01:10:28.250 --> 01:10:29.889
Thank you very much.

01:10:29.889 --> 01:10:35.699
MOHAN: Look, I think the scale and scope of
Chinese activity in the Indian Ocean, I mean,

01:10:35.699 --> 01:10:38.420
both economic-not all of it is military.

01:10:38.420 --> 01:10:42.540
Quite a bit of it is, as you pointed out,
huge amount of infrastructure investments.

01:10:42.540 --> 01:10:48.610
And I think a lot of has preceded the OBOR
campaign by President Xi Jinping, so it goes

01:10:48.610 --> 01:10:53.070
back certainly to the last 15 to 20 years.

01:10:53.070 --> 01:10:57.949
As the interest in the natural resources have
grown, the Chinese have come in a big way

01:10:57.949 --> 01:11:03.579
and I think have invested in east coast of
Africa, in the Horn of Africa, in southern

01:11:03.579 --> 01:11:04.579
Africa.

01:11:04.579 --> 01:11:10.130
So I think it's part much like what we saw
the European capitalist expansion in the 17th,

01:11:10.130 --> 01:11:15.159
18th, 19th centuries, or even what British
India did was to build these new ports in

01:11:15.159 --> 01:11:23.030
Hong Kong, in Singapore, the subcontinental
ports in Madras, Bombay, Calcutta, Aden.

01:11:23.030 --> 01:11:28.320
So I think what we're seeing is this dramatic
expansion of Chinese capital and its global

01:11:28.320 --> 01:11:32.909
footprint is reflecting itself in this new
infrastructure that they're building.

01:11:32.909 --> 01:11:37.159
So in a sense it's historically in tune with
what's happened before.

01:11:37.159 --> 01:11:40.280
The challenge is not that the Chinese are
bad and others were good.

01:11:40.280 --> 01:11:44.599
The fact is, today, the Chinese have that
capability, have the resources.

01:11:44.599 --> 01:11:48.059
Some people say they have excess industrial
capacity at home, so they're really merely

01:11:48.059 --> 01:11:49.059
deploying it.

01:11:49.059 --> 01:11:53.119
Whatever the reason is, the Chinese presence
in the economics of this region-that's where

01:11:53.119 --> 01:11:56.039
we began our discussion-that's a reality.

01:11:56.039 --> 01:11:58.179
Now, what can India do about it?

01:11:58.179 --> 01:12:06.290
Look, India too-I think there was a lot of
wariness, unhappiness in India in the past

01:12:06.290 --> 01:12:08.849
that-with the OBOR initiative, with other
things.

01:12:08.849 --> 01:12:12.949
But the present government, I think, has come
to a better judgment, in a sense.

01:12:12.949 --> 01:12:16.780
Look, we're not going to oppose everything
that the Chinese do in the region.

01:12:16.780 --> 01:12:20.920
In the case of China-Pakistan corridor, because
the sovereignty of Kashmir is involved, India

01:12:20.920 --> 01:12:22.190
is opposed to it.

01:12:22.190 --> 01:12:26.710
But where Chinese-we are negotiating with
Chinese on building a different corridor in

01:12:26.710 --> 01:12:31.199
the eastern part of the subcontinent, through
Myanmar, Bangladesh, and India.

01:12:31.199 --> 01:12:35.459
So my sense is, where there is a potential
for collaboration with the Chinese, I think

01:12:35.459 --> 01:12:36.459
India is quite open.

01:12:36.459 --> 01:12:40.960
But more interestingly, I think what India
is doing is India is working with Japan and

01:12:40.960 --> 01:12:42.039
the United States.

01:12:42.039 --> 01:12:46.239
If you look at the Obama-Modi statement, that
they talked about an Indo-Pacific corridor.

01:12:46.239 --> 01:12:51.840
India and Japan are very eager to work together
to develop the infrastructure in this part

01:12:51.840 --> 01:12:53.590
of the-in this part of the world.

01:12:53.590 --> 01:12:57.699
Those plans are in the-I would say far less
advanced than the Chinese.

01:12:57.699 --> 01:13:01.969
But I think what it does, this competition
for infrastructure, actually gives options

01:13:01.969 --> 01:13:05.230
to countries like Djibouti that you can happily
bargain.

01:13:05.230 --> 01:13:09.980
I mean, this was the original intent of, I
think, the idea of nonalignment.

01:13:09.980 --> 01:13:13.739
I think that is a great recourse today, because
I think the presence of the Chinese has given

01:13:13.739 --> 01:13:14.739
you options.

01:13:14.739 --> 01:13:18.179
And if you see what the Japanese have done-in
fact, the Japanese initiative, because they

01:13:18.179 --> 01:13:21.389
don't have a Japanese communist party running
that country, so their campaign is not as

01:13:21.389 --> 01:13:22.389
good.

01:13:22.389 --> 01:13:25.359
But they have something called the PQI, I
mean, if you can remember that-Partnership

01:13:25.359 --> 01:13:26.459
for Quality Infrastructure.

01:13:26.459 --> 01:13:31.329
Abe has put $110 billion on the line.

01:13:31.329 --> 01:13:35.010
In fact, my sense is they're going to bring
much better terms.

01:13:35.010 --> 01:13:37.690
Like India, they've given this high-speed
train.

01:13:37.690 --> 01:13:44.000
They've given us, what, billions of dollars,
given at 0.01 percent interest rate, repayable

01:13:44.000 --> 01:13:45.670
over 50 years.

01:13:45.670 --> 01:13:48.099
There's no way the Chinese are going to be
able to match those terms.

01:13:48.099 --> 01:13:52.079
So there is huge potential for developing
alternate frameworks.

01:13:52.079 --> 01:13:57.290
So rather than opposing what the Chinese do,
I think it is possible to construct alternate

01:13:57.290 --> 01:13:58.290
frameworks.

01:13:58.290 --> 01:14:01.559
And I think that will force the Chinese, I
think eventually, to offer better terms.

01:14:01.559 --> 01:14:06.820
So my sense is the game is on, so let's take
advantage.

01:14:06.820 --> 01:14:13.150
INDERFURTH: I think Raja's comment about the
game is on could be a nice way to conclude.

01:14:13.150 --> 01:14:17.110
The game is on in Southern Asia.

01:14:17.110 --> 01:14:21.280
There is a transforming geopolitical landscape.

01:14:21.280 --> 01:14:28.550
Clearly the rise of China and India are a
major part of that; the role that Pakistan

01:14:28.550 --> 01:14:29.550
plays.

01:14:29.550 --> 01:14:34.000
One thing that I come away from this is I
think we've had a good reality check on the

01:14:34.000 --> 01:14:36.330
major flashpoints.

01:14:36.330 --> 01:14:41.150
We've spoken of those that are most commonly
referred to, whether it be maritime-South

01:14:41.150 --> 01:14:46.309
China Sea, Indian Ocean-nuclear issues, the
question of the spread of ISIS.

01:14:46.309 --> 01:14:51.929
I think we've gotten a really good reality
check on where these stand today, what our

01:14:51.929 --> 01:14:53.300
concerns should be.

01:14:53.300 --> 01:14:58.229
So I want to conclude the session by saying,
on behalf of the Council on Foreign Relations,

01:14:58.229 --> 01:15:00.559
thank you all for being here.

01:15:00.559 --> 01:15:07.300
And I think it only remains to express our
appreciation to this excellent panel for all

01:15:07.300 --> 01:15:08.949
their comments and their time.

01:15:08.949 --> 01:15:09.949
So thank you all very much.

01:15:09.949 --> 01:15:10.949
(Applause.)

01:15:10.949 --> 01:15:10.950
(END)

